OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026", "title": "Homogeneous depth-five lower bounds for iterated matrix multiplication", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026.pdf", "family": "135", "family_title": "Homogeneous depth-five lower bounds for iterated matrix multiplication", "abstract_html": "Let \\(\\mathop{\\mathrm{IMM}}\\nolimits _{n,n}\\) be the \\((1,1)\\) entry of a product of <i>n</i> independent \\(n\\times n\\) matrices of variables. Over every field of characteristic zero, every syntactically homogeneous \\(\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\) circuit computing \\(\\mathop{\\mathrm{IMM}}\\nolimits _{n,n}\\) has at least \\(n^{\\sqrt n/400}\\) gates for all sufficiently large <i>n</i>, with an absolute threshold independent of the field. Bottom linear forms may have arbitrary support, and arbitrary finite fan-in, fan-out, and gate sharing are allowed. Over every field, a block expansion gives such circuits with at most \\(n^{\\sqrt n+4}\\) gates for <i>n</i> ≥ 2. Thus the gate complexity over characteristic-zero fields is \\(n^{\\Theta(\\sqrt n)}\\).", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist absolute constants \\(c>0\\) and \\(n_0\\) such that, for every integer \\(n\\geq n_0\\), every syntactically homogeneous \\(\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\) circuit over \\(\\mathbb C\\) computing …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For integers \\(w,d\\geq 1\\), let \\(X^{(1)},\\ldots,X^{(d)}\\) be \\(w\\times w\\) matrices whose entries are distinct commuting variables. Define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{IMM}_{w,d}\n  =\\bigl(X^{(1)}\\cdots X^{(d)}\\bigr)_{1,1}\n  =\\sum_{i_1,\\ldots,i_{d-1}\\in[w]}\n  x^{(1)}_{1,i_1}x^{(2)}_{i_1,i_2}\\cdots x^{(d)}_{i_{d-1},1}.\n  \\]\n  For \\(d=1\\), this means \\(x^{(1)}_{1,1}\\). All \\(dw^2\\) matrix-entry variables are retained as ambient variables, including entries absent from the polynomial.</p><p class=\"li\">An arithmetic circuit over a field \\(K\\) is a finite directed acyclic graph. Leaves are variables or elements of \\(K\\). A sum gate computes a \\(K\\)-linear combination of its inputs, and a product gate computes their product. Fan-in and fan-out are arbitrary and finite. Gates may be shared. Repeated input occurrences at a product gate are counted with multiplicity. Scalar coefficients on sum inputs require no additional gates.</p><p class=\"li\">A \\(\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\) circuit has five computational layers, from output to leaves, of types \\(+,\\times,+,\\times,+\\), with one output gate. Edges between computational gates join consecutive layers, and bottom sums read leaves. Unary gates are allowed. Bottom linear forms have unrestricted support.</p><p class=\"li\">The circuit is syntactically homogeneous if variables have formal degree \\(1\\), constants have formal degree \\(0\\), product gates add input degrees with multiplicity, and all inputs of a sum gate have the same formal degree. Empty sums and products, if present, compute \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) and have formal degree \\(0\\). Cancellation does not reset formal degree.</p><p class=\"li\">Size is the number of vertices, including leaves. Computational-gate count excludes leaves; wire size counts input occurrences. All computations concern formal polynomials.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist absolute constants \\(c&gt;0\\) and \\(n_0\\) such that, for every integer \\(n\\geq n_0\\), every syntactically homogeneous \\(\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\) circuit over \\(\\mathbb C\\) computing \\(\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n}\\) has size at least\n\\[\nn^{c\\sqrt n}.\n\\]\nOne may take \\(c=1/400\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let a syntactically homogeneous \\(\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\) circuit over \\(\\mathbb C\\) of size \\(S\\) compute\n\\[\nf=\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n}.\n\\]\nUse the partition from Proposition 3. For all sufficiently large \\(n\\), Propositions 3 and 2 give absolute constants \\(C_1,C_2&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\nD\\exp(-C_1\\sqrt n)\n\\leq R(f)\n\\leq\n2S^2D\\exp(C_2\\sqrt n)n^{-\\sqrt n/100}.\n\\]\nSince \\(D&gt;0\\), cancellation and square roots yield\n\\[\nS\\geq\n2^{-1/2}\n\\exp\\left(-\\frac{C_1+C_2}{2}\\sqrt n\\right)\nn^{\\sqrt n/200}.\n\\]</p><p>After increasing the absolute threshold for \\(n\\), the exponential factor and \\(2^{-1/2}\\) are absorbed by \\(n^{\\sqrt n/400}\\). Therefore\n\\[\nS\\geq n^{\\sqrt n/400},\n\\]\nas asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 6.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an absolute integer \\(n_0\\), independent of \\(K\\), such that, for every \\(n\\geq n_0\\), every such circuit over \\(K\\) computing \\(\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n}\\) has at least \\( n^{\\sqrt n/400} \\) gates.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\) be any field of characteristic zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n}\\), syntactically homogeneous \\(\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\) circuits, and gate size have the definitions in Theorem 1. In particular, all \\(n^3\\) matrix-entry variables remain ambient variables, bottom fan-in and bottom support are unrestricted, and arbitrary finite fan-out, repeated input occurrences, constants, and shared subcircuits are permitted.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute integer \\(n_0\\), independent of \\(K\\), such that, for every \\(n\\geq n_0\\), every such circuit over \\(K\\) computing \\(\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n}\\) has at least\n\\[\nn^{\\sqrt n/400}\n\\]\ngates.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(n\\) and the parameters and partition used in Proposition 3. Express \\(F_{\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n}}\\) in ordinary monomial bases of its domain and codomain. The coefficients of \\(\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n}\\) are integers, multiplication has integral matrix entries, and differentiation has integral matrix entries: a repeated derivative acts by a product of falling factorials of nonnegative integers. Consequently this finite matrix has entries in \\(\\mathbb Z\\).</p><p>Every minor is an integer. Under the natural map\n\\[\n\\mathbb Z\\longrightarrow K,\n\\]\nan integer is zero precisely when it was zero in \\(\\mathbb Z\\), because \\(K\\) has characteristic zero. The same holds over \\(\\mathbb C\\). Thus the largest order of a nonzero minor, and hence the rank, is the same over \\(K\\) and over \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p>Changing from ordinary monomials to the normalized bases used in the complex moment argument does not change that complex rank. Proposition 3 therefore supplies its lower bound over \\(K\\). Proposition 2 holds over every field, with the same constants. Comparing these bounds gives\n\\[\nD\\exp(-C_1\\sqrt n)\n\\leq\n2S^2D\\exp(C_2\\sqrt n)n^{-\\sqrt n/100}.\n\\]\nThe cancellation, square-root estimate, and absorption used in the proof of Theorem 1 give\n\\[\nS\\geq n^{\\sqrt n/400}\n\\]\nfor an absolute threshold independent of \\(K\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Proposition 6.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a syntactically homogeneous \\(\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\) circuit over \\(K\\) computing \\(\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n}\\) whose number of gates, including leaves, is at most …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\) be any field and let \\(n\\geq 2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the polynomial and circuit definitions in Theorem 1, with gates counted including leaves.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  t=\\lceil\\sqrt n\\rceil,\\qquad r=\\left\\lceil\\frac nt\\right\\rceil.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a syntactically homogeneous \\(\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\) circuit over \\(K\\) computing \\(\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n}\\) whose number of gates, including leaves, is at most\n\\[\n2n^3+rn^2+rn^{t+1}+n^{r-1}+1\n\\leq n^{\\sqrt n+4}.\n\\tag{29}\n\\]\nThe construction is homogeneous of depth four before unary bottom sums are inserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Partition the \\(n\\) consecutive matrix layers into \\(r\\) nonempty consecutive blocks, with lengths\n\\[\n\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_r\\leq t.\n\\]\nFor block \\(j\\), let \\(Y^{(j)}\\) be the product of its matrices. An entry \\(Y^{(j)}_{a,b}\\) is a sum of \\(n^{\\ell_j-1}\\) monomials, one for each choice of the internal indices of that block, and each monomial has degree \\(\\ell_j\\).</p><p>At the bottom sum layer, use one identity sum gate for each variable, sharing it among all its uses. This requires at most \\(n^3\\) leaves and \\(n^3\\) bottom sum gates. For every block and each of its \\(n^2\\) entries, compute the displayed monomials by lower product gates and add them at a middle sum gate. Thus block \\(j\\) uses\n\\[\nn^{\\ell_j+1}\n\\]\nlower product gates and \\(n^2\\) middle sum gates. When \\(\\ell_j=1\\), the product and sum gates have one input; all five computational layers are still present.</p><p>Set \\(a_0=a_r=1\\). Matrix multiplication now gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n}\n=\n\\sum_{(a_1,\\ldots,a_{r-1})\\in[n]^{r-1}}\n\\prod_{j=1}^rY^{(j)}_{a_{j-1},a_j}.\n\\]\nFor \\(r=1\\), the right-hand side means the single entry \\(Y^{(1)}_{1,1}\\). Use one upper product gate for each of the \\(n^{r-1}\\) summands, and one output sum gate. Each block-entry gate is shared by all summands that use it.</p><p>The upper products have formal degree\n\\[\n\\ell_1+\\cdots+\\ell_r=n,\n\\]\nand every middle sum adds monomials of its block’s degree. The bottom sums have degree one, so the whole circuit is syntactically homogeneous. Any gates that do not feed the output may be discarded.</p><p>The gate count is at most\n\\[\n2n^3+rn^2+\\sum_{j=1}^r n^{\\ell_j+1}+n^{r-1}+1,\n\\]\nwhich proves the first bound in (29).</p><p>For the second, note that \\(r\\leq t\\leq n\\). If \\(n\\geq3\\), then \\(t\\geq2\\) and the first bound is at most\n\\[\n3n^3+n^{t+2}+n^{t-1}+1\n\\leq3n^{t+2}\n\\leq n^{t+3}\n\\leq n^{\\sqrt n+4}.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n3n^3\\leq n^{t+2},\n\\qquad\nn^{t-1}+1\\leq n^{t+2}\n\\]\njustify the middle estimate. For \\(n=2\\), the first bound is \\(30\\leq2^5\\) and \\(t=2\\), giving the same conclusion.</p><p>The bottom sums are unary identity gates; omitting them gives the stated homogeneous depth-four construction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For all sufficiently large \\(n\\), \\( \\frac{\\sqrt n}{2}\\leq s\\leq\\sqrt n,\\qquad \\lambda\\geq\\frac38,\\qquad 0<\\rho<1, \\) \\( v,u=\\Theta(n^3),\\qquad a,b=\\Theta(n^{5/2}), \\) \\( a\\geq k,\\qquad 2n\\leq\\frac12\\min(a,b), \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Throughout, \\(n\\) is a sufficiently large positive integer. All implicit constants and thresholds are absolute.</p><p class=\"li\">For integers \\(r\\geq 1\\) and \\(t\\geq 0\\), define\n  \\[\n  h(r,t)=\\binom{r+t-1}{t}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(s\\) be the largest integer at most \\(\\sqrt n\\) having the same parity as \\(n\\). Define\n  \\[\n  k=\\frac{n-s}{2},\\qquad\n  m=\\frac{n+s}{2},\\qquad\n  \\rho=\\frac{k}{m},\\qquad\n  \\lambda=\\frac{k}{n}=\\frac{\\rho}{1+\\rho},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  v=kn^2,\\qquad\n  u=mn^2,\\qquad\n  a=\\left\\lceil\\frac{v}{\\sqrt n-1}\\right\\rceil,\\qquad\n  \\alpha=\\frac{a}{a+v},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  b=\\left\\lceil\\frac{u\\alpha^\\rho}{1-\\alpha^\\rho}\\right\\rceil,\\qquad\n  \\beta=\\frac{b}{b+u},\\qquad\n  q=\\alpha^{(1+\\rho)/2},\\qquad\n  D=h(v,a)h(u,b).\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large \\(n\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt n}{2}\\leq s\\leq\\sqrt n,\\qquad\n\\lambda\\geq\\frac38,\\qquad\n0&lt;\\rho&lt;1,\n\\]\n\\[\nv,u=\\Theta(n^3),\\qquad a,b=\\Theta(n^{5/2}),\n\\]\n\\[\na\\geq k,\\qquad\n2n\\leq\\frac12\\min(a,b),\n\\]\n\\[\nn^{-1/2}\\leq\\alpha\n=n^{-1/2}\\exp(O(1/a)),\\qquad\n\\alpha^{-1}\\leq\\sqrt n,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha^\\rho\\leq\\beta\n\\leq\\alpha^\\rho\\exp(C/b),\\qquad\n\\beta\\leq\\frac2{\\sqrt n},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nq=n^{-1/2}\\exp\\bigl(O(s\\log n/n+1/a)\\bigr),\\qquad\nn^{-1/2}\\leq q\\leq\\frac2{\\sqrt n}\\leq n^{-2/5},\\qquad\nq\\leq\\frac12,\n\\]\nwhere \\(C\\) is an absolute constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>The parity choice gives\n\\[\n\\sqrt n-2&lt;s\\leq\\sqrt n,\n\\]\nand hence the claims about \\(s,k,m,\\lambda,\\rho,v,u\\).</p><p>Write\n\\[\na_0=\\frac{v}{\\sqrt n-1},\n\\]\nso that \\(a_0\\leq a&lt;a_0+1\\) and\n\\[\n\\frac{a_0}{a_0+v}=n^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nThe function \\(x/(x+v)\\) is increasing, and the derivative of its logarithm is\n\\[\n\\frac{v}{x(x+v)}\\leq\\frac1x.\n\\]\nThe mean value theorem therefore gives\n\\[\n0\\leq\\log(\\alpha n^{1/2})\\leq\\frac1{a_0}=O(1/a).\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(a=\\Theta(n^{5/2})\\) and \\(\\alpha^{-1}\\leq\\sqrt n\\).</p><p>Since\n\\[\n1-\\rho=\\frac{s}{m}=O(s/n),\n\\]\nthe preceding estimate implies\n\\[\n\\alpha^\\rho\n=n^{-1/2}\\exp\\bigl(O(s\\log n/n+1/a)\\bigr)\n=n^{-1/2}(1+o(1)).\n\\]</p><p>Put\n\\[\nb_0=\\frac{u\\alpha^\\rho}{1-\\alpha^\\rho}.\n\\]\nThen \\(b_0=\\Theta(n^{5/2})\\), \\(b_0\\leq b&lt;b_0+1\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{b_0}{b_0+u}=\\alpha^\\rho.\n\\]\nApplying the same logarithmic derivative bound with \\(u\\) in place of \\(v\\) gives\n\\[\n0\\leq\\log(\\beta/\\alpha^\\rho)\\leq\\frac1{b_0}\\leq\\frac Cb.\n\\]\nThis proves the assertions about \\(b,\\beta\\), including \\(\\beta\\leq2/\\sqrt n\\) eventually. The growth of \\(a,b\\) also gives\n\\[\na\\geq k,\\qquad 2n\\leq\\frac12\\min(a,b).\n\\]</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\log q\n=-\\frac{1+\\rho}{4}\\log n+O(1/a)\n=-\\frac12\\log n+O(s\\log n/n+1/a).\n\\]\nBecause \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1\\) and \\((1+\\rho)/2\\leq1\\), we have\n\\[\nq\\geq\\alpha\\geq n^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nThe remaining upper bounds follow from the last display and \\(s\\log n/n\\to0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are absolute constants \\(C>0\\) and \\(n_0\\) such that, for \\(n\\geq n_0\\), \\( R(g)\\leq 2S^2D\\exp(C\\sqrt n)\\,n^{-\\sqrt n/100}. \\) The constants and threshold are independent of the field, circuit, and partition.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\) be any field. Use the numerical parameters\n  \\[\n  s,k,m,\\rho,\\lambda,v,u,a,b,\\alpha,\\beta,q,D\n  \\]\n  defined in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Assign any \\(k\\) full matrix layers to a variable set \\(V\\), and the other \\(m\\) layers to a disjoint variable set \\(U\\). Retain all \\(n^2\\) variables of each layer, including variables absent from the endpoint entry. Thus \\(|V|=v\\), \\(|U|=u\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a variable set \\(E\\) and an integer \\(t\\geq0\\), let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal H_E(t)=K[E]_t,\\qquad\n  \\dim\\mathcal H_E(t)=h(|E|,t).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(Q\\in K[V,U]\\), define\n  \\[\n  T_Q=Q(\\partial_V,U),\n  \\]\n  replacing variables in \\(V\\) by formal partial derivatives and variables in \\(U\\) by multiplication operators. These operators commute, since \\(V\\) and \\(U\\) are disjoint, and\n  \\[\n  T_{Q_1Q_2}=T_{Q_1}T_{Q_2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a homogeneous degree-\\(n\\) polynomial \\(g\\), let \\(g_{[k,m]}\\) be its component of \\(V\\)-degree \\(k\\) and \\(U\\)-degree \\(m\\). Define\n  \\[\n  F_g=g_{[k,m]}(\\partial_V,U):\n  \\mathcal H_V(a)\\otimes\\mathcal H_U(b)\n  \\longrightarrow\n  \\mathcal H_V(a-k)\\otimes\\mathcal H_U(b+m),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  R(g)=\\operatorname{rank}F_g.\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n  The source has dimension \\(D=h(v,a)h(u,b)\\). Projection and substitution are linear, so\n  \\[\n  R(g_1+g_2)\\leq R(g_1)+R(g_2),\\qquad\n  R(cg)=R(g)\\quad(c\\in K\\setminus\\{0\\}),\\qquad R(0)=0.\n  \\]\n  The map \\(g\\mapsto F_g\\) includes a fixed bidegree projection and is not asserted to be multiplicative.</p><p class=\"li\">For every positive integer \\(e\\), define\n  \\[\n  H_e=\\sum_{i=0}^e q^{|i-\\lambda e|},\\qquad\n  d_e=\\operatorname{dist}(\\lambda e,\\mathbb Z),\\qquad\n  t_0=\\frac{n}{4s}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g\\in K[V,U]\\) be homogeneous of degree \\(n\\), computed by a syntactically homogeneous \\(\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\) circuit of size \\(S\\), with the circuit model of Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are absolute constants \\(C&gt;0\\) and \\(n_0\\) such that, for \\(n\\geq n_0\\),\n\\[\nR(g)\\leq 2S^2D\\exp(C\\sqrt n)\\,n^{-\\sqrt n/100}.\n\\]\nThe constants and threshold are independent of the field, circuit, and partition.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(g=0\\), the conclusion is immediate. Otherwise its formal output degree is \\(n\\): induction on the gates shows that every nonzero polynomial at a formally homogeneous degree-\\(d\\) gate is homogeneous of degree \\(d\\).</p><p>We first record exactly what the circuit layers provide. A bottom sum gate computes either a homogeneous linear form or a scalar, because all its leaf inputs have the same formal degree. A lower product of formal degree \\(e&gt;0\\) therefore computes a scalar times a product of exactly \\(e\\) homogeneous linear-form occurrences. Degree-zero factors are absorbed into that scalar. A zero factor makes the whole product zero and its contribution can be discarded. A gate computing zero need not be assigned a different formal degree.</p><p>At either a middle sum or the output sum, repeated wires from the same predecessor gate can be combined by adding their scalar coefficients. There are at most \\(S\\) distinct predecessors. Thus \\(g\\) is a sum of at most \\(S\\) scalar multiples of products\n\\[\nG=\\prod_{j=1}^r Q_j,\\qquad\n\\deg Q_j=e_j\\geq1,\\qquad\n\\sum_j e_j=n,\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nand each \\(Q_j\\) is a sum of at most \\(S\\) scalar multiples of products of \\(e_j\\) homogeneous linear forms. Zero upper terms have been discarded, and degree-zero factors in the remaining terms have been absorbed into their coefficients. If empty sums or products are allowed, they contribute only zero or a scalar and are handled in the same way.</p><p>In (9), inputs to a product are counted with multiplicity: repeated use of a gate gives repeated factors. Their positive degrees add to \\(n\\), so \\(r\\leq n\\). Shared subcircuits do not affect the bounds on the number of distinct additive predecessors. In particular, this description does not charge for wires or impose any restriction on the support of a linear form.</p><p>Suppose first that every \\(e_j&lt;t_0\\). By Lemma 2.2,\n\\[\n\\sum_j d_{e_j}\n\\geq\\frac{s}{2n}\\sum_j e_j\n=\\frac s2.\n\\]\nApply Lemmas 2.1 and 2.2, weakening \\(q^{d_{e_j}}\\) to \\(q^{d_{e_j}/2}\\) for even degrees. The combined even-degree correction is at most \\(\\exp(4C_1\\sqrt n)\\). Consequently\n\\[\nR(G)\n\\leq2D\\exp(4C_1\\sqrt n)q^{s/4}\n\\leq2D\\exp(4C_1\\sqrt n)n^{-\\sqrt n/20},\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nwhere we used \\(q\\leq n^{-2/5}\\) and \\(s\\geq\\sqrt n/2\\).</p><p>Now suppose that one factor occurrence has degree \\(e\\geq t_0\\). Expand that occurrence, using its middle sum, into at most \\(S\\) products of \\(e\\) homogeneous linear forms. All other factors remain unexpanded. This includes any other occurrences of the same gate: expanding one occurrence in \\(Q^h\\) leaves its other \\(h-1\\) occurrences unexpanded.</p><p>In each resulting product, the \\(e\\) newly supplied linear factors contribute\n\\[\nH_1^e\\leq q^{\\lambda e/2}\n\\]\nto the estimate of Lemma 2.1, because \\(d_1=\\lambda\\) and \\(1&lt;t_0\\). There are at most\n\\[\n\\frac n{t_0}=4s\n\\]\nremaining factor occurrences of degree at least \\(t_0\\), and their \\(H\\)-factors are at most \\(5\\) each. The remaining low odd degrees contribute at most \\(1\\). The remaining low even degrees contribute at most \\(\\exp(4C_1\\sqrt n)\\) after their favorable powers of \\(q\\) are dropped. Rank subadditivity over the expansion therefore yields\n\\[\nR(G)\n\\leq2SD\\,5^{4s}\\exp(4C_1\\sqrt n)q^{\\lambda t_0/2}\n\\leq2SD\\,5^{4s}\\exp(4C_1\\sqrt n)n^{-3\\sqrt n/160}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nFor the last step, \\(q\\leq n^{-2/5}\\), \\(\\lambda\\geq3/8\\), and \\(t_0\\geq\\sqrt n/4\\) imply\n\\[\n\\frac{\\lambda t_0}{5}\\geq\\frac{3\\sqrt n}{160}.\n\\]</p><p>Since\n\\[\n5^{4s}\\leq\\exp(4\\log(5)\\sqrt n),\n\\]\nboth (10) and (11) are at most\n\\[\n2SD\\exp(C\\sqrt n)n^{-\\sqrt n/100}\n\\]\nfor an absolute \\(C\\). Multiplication by a nonzero scalar leaves rank unchanged, and multiplication by zero gives rank zero. Summing the at most \\(S\\) upper-product contributions proves the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( R\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^r Q_j\\right) \\leq 2D\\prod_{j=1}^r H_{e_j}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In the setting of Proposition 2, let \\(Q_1,\\ldots,Q_r\\in K[V,U]\\) be homogeneous of respective positive degrees \\(e_1,\\ldots,e_r\\), with\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{j=1}^r e_j=n.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nR\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^r Q_j\\right)\n\\leq 2D\\prod_{j=1}^r H_{e_j}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Decompose each factor into its bidegree components. The component of their product that defines the rank measure is\n\\[\n\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^r Q_j\\right)_{[k,m]}\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{0\\leq i_j\\leq e_j\\ (1\\leq j\\leq r)\\\\\n\\sum_j i_j=k}}\n\\prod_{j=1}^r(Q_j)_{[i_j,e_j-i_j]}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix an assignment in this sum, and put\n\\[\n\\delta_j=i_j-\\lambda e_j.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\lambda n=k\\), we have \\(\\sum_j\\delta_j=0\\). The operators obtained by substituting \\(\\partial_V\\) and \\(U\\) commute, so we may apply first the factors with \\(\\delta_j&gt;0\\). Write their total bidegree as\n\\[\nI=\\sum_{\\delta_j&gt;0}i_j,\\qquad\nJ=\\sum_{\\delta_j&gt;0}(e_j-i_j).\n\\]</p><p>The resulting intermediate space is\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_V(a-I)\\otimes\\mathcal H_U(b+J).\n\\]\nIt is well defined because \\(I\\leq k\\leq a\\); also \\(J\\leq m\\). Its dimension bounds the rank of this contribution, including when the set of positive discrepancies is empty.</p><p>The successive ratios of binomial coefficients give\n\\[\n\\frac{h(v,a-I)}{h(v,a)}\n=\n\\prod_{t=0}^{I-1}\\frac{a-t}{a+v-1-t}\n\\leq\n\\left(\\frac{a}{a+v-1}\\right)^I,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{h(u,b+J)}{h(u,b)}\n=\n\\prod_{t=1}^{J}\\frac{b+u+t-1}{b+t}\n\\leq\n\\left(\\frac{b+u}{b}\\right)^J\n=\\beta^{-J}.\n\\]\nThe first inequality follows because \\(x/(x+v-1)\\) is nondecreasing for \\(x&gt;0\\). Since \\(\\beta\\geq\\alpha^\\rho\\), these inequalities imply\n\\[\n\\frac{h(v,a-I)h(u,b+J)}{D}\n\\leq\n\\alpha^{I-\\rho J}\n\\left(1-\\frac1{a+v}\\right)^{-I}\n\\leq 2\\alpha^{I-\\rho J}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nThe last bound holds uniformly for \\(I\\leq n\\), because \\(a+v=\\Theta(n^3)\\). Empty products in the ratio formulas equal \\(1\\).</p><p>Using \\(\\lambda=\\rho/(1+\\rho)\\) and \\(\\sum_j\\delta_j=0\\), we obtain\n\\[\nI-\\rho J\n=(1+\\rho)\\sum_{\\delta_j&gt;0}\\delta_j\n=\\frac{1+\\rho}{2}\\sum_j|\\delta_j|.\n\\]\nAs \\(q=\\alpha^{(1+\\rho)/2}\\), the rank of the fixed-assignment contribution is therefore at most\n\\[\n2D\\prod_j q^{|i_j-\\lambda e_j|}.\n\\]</p><p>Rank subadditivity permits summing these bounds. Dropping the constraint \\(\\sum_j i_j=k\\) increases the resulting nonnegative sum and makes it factor as\n\\[\n2D\\prod_j H_{e_j}.\n\\]\nZero bidegree components contribute rank zero throughout.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every integer \\(e\\geq1\\), \\( H_e\\leq q^{d_e}\\bigl(1+4q^{1-2d_e}\\bigr)\\leq5. \\) If \\(1\\leq e<t_0\\), then …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting and definitions of Proposition 2:\n  \\[\n  H_e=\\sum_{i=0}^e q^{|i-\\lambda e|},\\qquad\n  d_e=\\operatorname{dist}(\\lambda e,\\mathbb Z),\\qquad\n  t_0=\\frac{n}{4s}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(e\\geq1\\),\n\\[\nH_e\\leq q^{d_e}\\bigl(1+4q^{1-2d_e}\\bigr)\\leq5.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nIf \\(1\\leq e&lt;t_0\\), then\n\\[\nd_e=\n\\begin{cases}\nse/(2n),&amp;e\\text{ even},\\\\{}[2mm]\n1/2-se/(2n),&amp;e\\text{ odd},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nd_e\\geq\\frac{se}{2n}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nIn this range, odd degrees satisfy\n\\[\nH_e\\leq q^{d_e/2},\n\\]\nwhereas even degrees satisfy\n\\[\nH_e\\leq q^{d_e}\\exp\\bigl(4q^{1-se/n}\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nThere is an absolute constant \\(C_1\\) such that, for every finite list of positive integers \\(e_1,\\ldots,e_r\\) with \\(\\sum_j e_j\\leq n\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{j:\\ e_j&lt;t_0\\\\e_j\\text{ even}}}\nq^{1-se_j/n}\n\\leq C_1\\sqrt n.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Enlarge the sum defining \\(H_e\\) to all integers. Apart from one nearest integer to \\(\\lambda e\\), the terms have total at most\n\\[\n\\frac{2q^{1-d_e}}{1-q}.\n\\]\nThis remains valid when the nearest integer is not unique. Since \\(q\\leq1/2\\), it gives the first inequality in (5); the second follows from \\(0\\leq d_e\\leq1/2\\) and \\(0&lt;q&lt;1\\).</p><p>For \\(e&lt;t_0\\), write\n\\[\n\\lambda e=\\frac e2-\\frac{se}{2n},\\qquad\n0&lt;\\frac{se}{2n}&lt;\\frac18.\n\\]\nThe nearest integer is consequently \\(e/2\\) for even \\(e\\), and \\((e-1)/2\\) for odd \\(e\\). This proves (6). For odd \\(e\\), we have \\(d_e\\geq3/8\\). By \\(q\\leq n^{-2/5}\\),\n\\[\nq^{-d_e/2}\\geq n^{d_e/5}\\geq n^{3/40}\\geq5\n\\]\nafter increasing the absolute threshold. Thus\n\\[\nH_e\\leq5q^{d_e}\\leq q^{d_e/2}.\n\\]\nFor even \\(e\\), substitute \\(2d_e=se/n\\) in (5) and use \\(1+x\\leq\\exp(x)\\) to obtain (7).</p><p>It remains to sum these even-degree errors. Increase the threshold so that \\(t_0&gt;2\\), and for real \\(z\\in[2,t_0]\\) define\n\\[\nf(z)=\\frac{q^{1-sz/n}}{z}.\n\\]\nIts logarithm is convex, since\n\\[\n(\\log f)''(z)=\\frac1{z^2}&gt;0.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nf(z)\\leq\\max\\{f(2),f(t_0)\\}.\n\\]\nThe sharp estimate for \\(q\\) in Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\nf(2)\n=\\frac12n^{-1/2}\\exp\\bigl(O(s\\log n/n+1/a)\\bigr)\n=O(n^{-1/2}).\n\\]\nAt the other endpoint, \\(s\\leq\\sqrt n\\) and \\(q\\leq2n^{-1/2}\\) give\n\\[\nf(t_0)=\\frac{4s}{n}q^{3/4}=O(n^{-7/8}).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nq^{1-se/n}\\leq\\frac{C_1e}{\\sqrt n}\n\\]\nuniformly over the low even degrees. Summing and using \\(\\sum_j e_j\\leq n\\) proves (8).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 4.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an absolute constant \\(C\\) such that, for all sufficiently large \\(n\\), \\( R(\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n})\\geq D\\exp(-C\\sqrt n). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work over \\(\\mathbb C\\), with \\(n\\) sufficiently large and with the numerical parameters\n  \\[\n  s,k,m,\\rho,\\lambda,v,u,a,b,\\alpha,\\beta,q,D\n  \\]\n  defined in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Arrange the matrix-layer groups in the order\n  \\[\n  VU^{\\ell_1}VU^{\\ell_2}\\cdots VU^{\\ell_k},\n  \\qquad\n  \\ell_i=1+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{is}{k}\\right\\rfloor\n  -\\left\\lfloor\\frac{(i-1)s}{k}\\right\\rfloor.\n  \\tag{12}\n  \\]\n  For sufficiently large \\(n\\), \\(0&lt;s/k&lt;1\\), so every \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1\\) or \\(2\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{i=1}^k\\ell_i=k+s=m.\n  \\]\n  Thus there are exactly \\(k\\) layers in \\(V\\), \\(m\\) layers in \\(U\\), and no adjacent \\(V\\)-layers. All \\(n^2\\) variables of each matrix layer are retained.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(f=\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n}\\) and \\(L=n-1\\). A path is\n  \\[\n  P=(p_0,\\ldots,p_n),\\qquad\n  p_0=p_n=1,\\qquad p_1,\\ldots,p_{n-1}\\in[n],\n  \\]\n  with layer coordinate\n  \\[\n  E_t(P)=x^{(t)}_{p_{t-1},p_t}.\n  \\]\n  There are \\(n^L\\) paths. The polynomial \\(f\\) has bidegree \\((k,m)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  F_f=f(\\partial_V,U):\n  \\mathcal H_V(a)\\otimes\\mathcal H_U(b)\n  \\longrightarrow\n  \\mathcal H_V(a-k)\\otimes\\mathcal H_U(b+m),\n  \\qquad\n  R(f)=\\operatorname{rank}F_f,\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\mathcal H_E(t)=\\mathbb C[E]_t\\). The source dimension is\n  \\[\n  D=h(v,a)h(u,b).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Give each homogeneous polynomial space the inner product for which\n  \\[\n  \\frac{z^M}{\\sqrt{M!}},\\qquad |M|=t,\\qquad M!=\\prod_i M_i!,\n  \\]\n  is an orthonormal basis, and use tensor-product inner products. Put\n  \\[\n  T=\\operatorname{tr}(F_f^*F_f),\\qquad\n  T_2=\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl((F_f^*F_f)^2\\bigr).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A uniformly chosen domain basis index \\(M\\) consists of two independent exponent vectors: one uniform among weak compositions of \\(a\\) into \\(v\\) parts, and one uniform among weak compositions of \\(b\\) into \\(u\\) parts. Define\n  \\[\n  A=\\frac av,\\qquad B=\\frac bu,\\qquad\n  \\mu=A^k(1+B)^m.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute constant \\(C\\) such that, for all sufficiently large \\(n\\),\n\\[\nR(\\operatorname{IMM}_{n,n})\\geq D\\exp(-C\\sqrt n).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.4, applied to Lemma 3.3, gives\n\\[\nT_2\n\\leq\nDn^{2L}\\mu^2(1+\\beta)^m\\exp(C\\sqrt n)\n\\leq\nDn^{2L}\\mu^2\\exp(C'\\sqrt n),\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\beta\\leq2/\\sqrt n\\) and \\(m\\leq n\\). By Lemma 3.2,\n\\[\nT\\geq Dn^L\\mu\\exp(-C''n^{-1/2})&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>For the positive semidefinite matrix\n\\[\nH=F_f^*F_f,\n\\]\nthe Cauchy–Schwarz inequality applied to its nonzero eigenvalues yields\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{tr}H)^2\n\\leq\n\\operatorname{rank}(H)\\operatorname{tr}(H^2).\n\\]\nSince \\(T&gt;0\\) and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(H)=\\operatorname{rank}(F_f)=R(f),\n\\]\nthe two moment bounds imply\n\\[\nR(f)\\geq\\frac{T^2}{T_2}\n\\geq D\\exp(-C'''\\sqrt n).\n\\]\nThis is the asserted lower bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\mathbb E\\prod_i(M_i)_{d_i} =\\frac{(t)_H}{r^{\\overline H}}\\prod_i d_i!. \\) The right side is zero when \\(H>t\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this occupation-moment statement, let \\(t\\geq0\\) and \\(r\\geq1\\) be integers, and let\n  \\[\n  M=(M_1,\\ldots,M_r)\n  \\]\n  be uniform among weak compositions of \\(t\\) into \\(r\\) parts.</p><p class=\"li\">For an integer \\(d\\geq0\\), write\n  \\[\n  (z)_d=z(z-1)\\cdots(z-d+1),\\qquad\n  r^{\\overline d}=r(r+1)\\cdots(r+d-1),\n  \\]\n  with both empty products equal to \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d_i\\geq0\\) be integers and put \\(H=\\sum_i d_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_r\\) be independent geometric random variables on the nonnegative integers with mean \\(G=t/r\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb E\\prod_i(M_i)_{d_i}\n=\\frac{(t)_H}{r^{\\overline H}}\\prod_i d_i!.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\nThe right side is zero when \\(H&gt;t\\).</p><p>If a polynomial \\(P\\) has nonnegative coefficients in the basis\n\\[\n\\left\\{\\prod_i(z_i)_{d_i}\\right\\},\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\mathbb EP(M)\\leq\\mathbb EP(Z).\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\nIf \\(t&gt;0\\) and all terms of that expansion have total order at most \\(H_0\\leq t/2\\), then also\n\\[\n\\mathbb EP(M)\n\\geq\n\\exp\\left(-\\frac{H_0^2}{t}-\\frac{H_0^2}{2r}\\right)\n\\mathbb EP(Z).\n\\tag{16}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(d\\geq0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\geq0}(j)_dy^j=\\frac{d!\\,y^d}{(1-y)^{d+1}}.\n\\]\nIf \\(H\\leq t\\), coefficient extraction therefore gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{|M|=t}\\prod_i(M_i)_{d_i}\n=\n\\left(\\prod_i d_i!\\right)\n\\binom{t+r-1}{t-H}.\n\\]\nDivide by \\(\\binom{t+r-1}{t}\\) to obtain (14). If \\(H&gt;t\\), every composition has \\(M_i&lt;d_i\\) for some \\(i\\), so every summand is zero, as is \\((t)_H\\).</p><p>For \\(G&gt;0\\), the geometric law is\n\\[\n\\Pr(Z_i=j)\n=\\frac1{1+G}\\left(\\frac G{1+G}\\right)^j\n\\qquad(j\\geq0),\n\\]\nand its factorial moment is\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(Z_i)_d=d!G^d.\n\\]\nFor \\(H\\leq t\\), the ratio of the moment in (14) to the corresponding independent geometric moment is\n\\[\n\\prod_{j=0}^{H-1}\\frac{1-j/t}{1+j/r}.\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\nIt is at most one. For \\(H&gt;t\\), the composition moment is zero, so the same upper comparison holds.</p><p>When \\(H\\leq H_0\\leq t/2\\), the inequalities\n\\[\n\\log(1-x)\\geq-2x\\quad(0\\leq x\\leq1/2),\n\\qquad\n\\log(1+x)\\leq x\\quad(x\\geq0)\n\\]\nshow that the logarithm of (17) is at least\n\\[\n-\\sum_{j=0}^{H-1}\\left(\\frac{2j}{t}+\\frac jr\\right)\n\\geq-\\frac{H_0^2}{t}-\\frac{H_0^2}{2r}.\n\\]\nSum these comparisons with the nonnegative coefficients of \\(P\\). If \\(t=0\\), both \\(M\\) and the geometric vector of mean zero are identically zero, giving (15) directly.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Section 4.2, “The first trace.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The path summand of \\(F_f\\) sends a normalized domain monomial indexed by \\(M\\) to the index \\(M+\\epsilon_P\\) with coefficient \\(c_P(M)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting, paths, normalized bases, uniform domain index \\(M\\), and quantities \\(T,A,B,\\mu,D,L\\) of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">For a path \\(P\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\epsilon_P\n  =-\\sum_{t:\\,V}e_{E_t(P)}\n  +\\sum_{t:\\,U}e_{E_t(P)},\n  \\]\n  where \\(e_i\\) is a coordinate unit vector.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  c_P(M)=\n  \\left(\n  \\prod_{t:\\,V}M_{E_t(P)}\n  \\prod_{t:\\,U}(M_{E_t(P)}+1)\n  \\right)^{1/2}.\n  \\tag{19}\n  \\]\n  A shift requiring a negative occupation contributes zero; coefficients are extended by zero to invalid input indices.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The path summand of \\(F_f\\) sends a normalized domain monomial indexed by \\(M\\) to the index \\(M+\\epsilon_P\\) with coefficient \\(c_P(M)\\). Moreover,\n\\[\nT\n=D\\sum_P\\mathbb E\n\\left[\n\\prod_{t:\\,V}M_{E_t(P)}\n\\prod_{t:\\,U}(M_{E_t(P)}+1)\n\\right],\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nT\\geq Dn^L\\mu\\exp(-O(n^{-1/2}))&gt;0.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The comparison in Lemma 3.1 is termwise and does not assert independence of the coordinates of \\(M\\). Apply it separately in the two variable groups. By Proposition 1, total orders at most \\(2n\\) satisfy its lower-bound hypothesis, and their combined logarithmic loss is\n\\[\nO\\left(\n\\frac{n^2}{a}+\\frac{n^2}{v}\n+\\frac{n^2}{b}+\\frac{n^2}{u}\n\\right)\n=O(n^{-1/2}).\n\\tag{18}\n\\]</p><p>In the chosen orthonormal bases, differentiation and multiplication act by\n\\[\n\\partial_i\\frac{z^M}{\\sqrt{M!}}\n=\n\\sqrt{M_i}\\,\n\\frac{z^{M-e_i}}{\\sqrt{(M-e_i)!}},\n\\]\n\\[\nz_i\\frac{z^M}{\\sqrt{M!}}\n=\n\\sqrt{M_i+1}\\,\n\\frac{z^{M+e_i}}{\\sqrt{(M+e_i)!}}.\n\\]\nThe derivative is zero when \\(M_i=0\\); its displayed expression is used only when \\(M_i\\geq1\\). In particular, repeated differentiation of order \\(d\\) has coefficient \\(\\sqrt{(M_i)_d}\\), with no additional factorial divisor.</p><p>Different layers have disjoint coordinates, so a path never repeats a coordinate. The displayed actions therefore give the shift \\(\\epsilon_P\\) and coefficient \\(c_P(M)\\). Moreover, the shifts \\(\\epsilon_P\\) are distinct: a shift specifies the coordinate in every layer and hence every vertex of \\(P\\).</p><p>Consequently different paths from the same input reach different output indices. Summing the squared entries of \\(F_f\\) gives the exact identity\n\\[\nT\n=D\\sum_P\\mathbb E\n\\left[\n\\prod_{t:\\,V}M_{E_t(P)}\n\\prod_{t:\\,U}(M_{E_t(P)}+1)\n\\right].\n\\]\nEvery polynomial inside this expectation has nonnegative falling-factorial coefficients. Under independent geometric occupations of means \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), its expectation is \\(\\mu\\). Lemma 3.1 and (18) therefore imply\n\\[\nT\\geq Dn^L\\mu\\exp(-O(n^{-1/2}))&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>The inactive endpoint coordinates are included in the uniform compositions and in \\(v,u\\) throughout; they introduce no additional factor into this calculation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Sections 4.3–4.4, “Four paths in the second trace” and “A sum over pairing types.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\frac{T_2}{Dn^{2L}\\mu^2} \\leq (1+\\beta)^m \\sum_{\\tau\\in\\{\\mathcal N,\\mathcal D\\}^n} n^{-w(\\tau)} \\alpha^{-d_V(\\tau)} \\beta^{d_U(\\tau)} \\prod_{t\\in N_V(\\tau)} \\bigl(1+\\alpha^{-1}n^{-h_t}\\bigr). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting and quantities \\(T_2,D,L,\\mu,\\alpha,\\beta,m\\) of Proposition 3 and the path coefficients and shifts of Lemma 3.2.</p><p class=\"li\">For a word \\(\\tau\\in\\{\\mathcal N,\\mathcal D\\}^n\\), define\n  \\[\n  w(\\tau)=\n  \\#\\{t\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n-1\\}:\\tau_t\\neq\\tau_{t+1}\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d_V(\\tau)\\) and \\(d_U(\\tau)\\) be the numbers of \\(\\mathcal D\\)-positions in the respective variable groups, and put\n  \\[\n  N_V(\\tau)=\\{t:t\\text{ is in }V,\\ \\tau_t=\\mathcal N\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a layer \\(t\\) of type \\(\\mathcal N\\), let \\(h_t\\) be the number of its end vertices that are internal and lie between two layers of type \\(\\mathcal N\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac{T_2}{Dn^{2L}\\mu^2}\n\\leq\n(1+\\beta)^m\n\\sum_{\\tau\\in\\{\\mathcal N,\\mathcal D\\}^n}\nn^{-w(\\tau)}\n\\alpha^{-d_V(\\tau)}\n\\beta^{d_U(\\tau)}\n\\prod_{t\\in N_V(\\tau)}\n\\bigl(1+\\alpha^{-1}n^{-h_t}\\bigr).\n\\tag{24}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The matrix entries of \\(F_f\\) in the chosen bases are real and nonnegative. An entry of \\(F_f^*F_f\\) indexed by \\(M,M'\\) is the sum of\n\\[\nc_P(M)c_Q(M')\n\\]\nover pairs of paths satisfying\n\\[\nM+\\epsilon_P=M'+\\epsilon_Q.\n\\]\nSquaring its entries and summing yields\n\\[\nT_2\n=\nD\\,\\mathbb E_M\n\\sum_{\\substack{P,Q,R,S:\\ \\epsilon_P-\\epsilon_Q=\\epsilon_R-\\epsilon_S\\\\\nM'=M+\\epsilon_P-\\epsilon_Q}}\nc_P(M)c_Q(M')c_R(M)c_S(M').\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\nThe zero convention handles invalid indices. There is no additional sum over \\(M'\\), because \\(M,P,Q\\) determine it.</p><p>The condition on four paths holds separately in each layer. Denoting their four coordinates in that layer by \\(p,q,r,s\\), respectively, it is\n\\[\ne_p-e_q=e_r-e_s.\n\\]\nIf this difference is zero, then \\(p=q\\) and \\(r=s\\). Otherwise its positive and negative coordinates force \\(p=r\\) and \\(q=s\\), with \\(p\\neq q\\). Thus there are exactly two types:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|l}\n\\mathcal N&amp;p=q=i,\\quad r=s=j,\\\\\n\\mathcal D&amp;p=r=i,\\quad q=s=j,\\quad i\\neq j.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe all-equal case belongs to \\(\\mathcal N\\).</p><p>For each compatible quadruple, the four-coefficient product in (21) is a product over layers of the following polynomials:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cc}\n&amp;\\mathcal N&amp;\\mathcal D\\\\ \\hline\nV&amp;M_iM_j&amp;M_i(M_j+1)\\\\\nU&amp;(M_i+1)(M_j+1)&amp;(M_i+1)M_j.\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\nIndeed, the shift from \\(M\\) to \\(M'\\) is zero in type \\(\\mathcal N\\). In a \\(V\\)-layer of type \\(\\mathcal D\\), it is \\(-e_i+e_j\\), so the two coefficients at \\(M\\) contribute \\(M_i\\) and the two at \\(M'\\) contribute \\(M_j+1\\). In a \\(U\\)-layer of type \\(\\mathcal D\\), the shift is \\(e_i-e_j\\), giving \\((M_i+1)M_j\\) instead.</p><p>These polynomial formulas also account for invalid indices. In type \\(\\mathcal D\\), a negative coordinate of \\(M'\\) requires \\(M_i=0\\) in a \\(V\\)-layer or \\(M_j=0\\) in a \\(U\\)-layer, and the corresponding table entry vanishes. In type \\(\\mathcal N\\), an unavailable derivative makes \\(M_iM_j\\) zero. Since different layers use distinct coordinates, these exhaust the possible failures. Thus the product in (22) equals the zero-extended contribution.</p><p>When \\(i=j\\) in type \\(\\mathcal N\\), the required expansions are\n\\[\nz^2=(z)_2+(z)_1,\\qquad\n(z+1)^2=(z)_2+3(z)_1+1.\n\\]\nAll other table entries are products of \\((z)_1\\) and \\((z)_1+1\\) on distinct coordinates. Hence every product of local entries has nonnegative falling-factorial coefficients, with order at most \\(2k\\) in \\(V\\) and \\(2m\\) in \\(U\\). Lemma 3.1 bounds its expectation by the expectation for independent geometric occupations.</p><p>For a geometric variable \\(Z\\) of mean \\(G\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb EZ^2=2G^2+G,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E(Z+1)^2=2G^2+3G+1.\n\\]\nDividing the geometric expectations in each \\(V\\)-layer by \\(A^2\\) and in each \\(U\\)-layer by \\((1+B)^2\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cc}\n&amp;\\mathcal N&amp;\\mathcal D\\\\ \\hline\nV&amp;1+\\alpha^{-1}\\mathbf1_{\\{i=j\\}}&amp;\\alpha^{-1}\\\\\nU&amp;1+\\beta\\mathbf1_{\\{i=j\\}}&amp;\\beta.\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n\\frac{A+1}{A}=\\alpha^{-1},\\qquad\n\\frac B{B+1}=\\beta.\n\\]\nFor example, the repeated-coordinate \\(V\\)-entry is\n\\[\n\\frac{2A^2+A}{A^2}=1+\\alpha^{-1},\n\\]\nso the repeated factorial contribution has been retained. The product of the normalizing factors over all layers is \\(\\mu^2\\).</p><p>Assign to a compatible quadruple its type word \\(\\tau\\). For each such word, enlarge its class of quadruples by dropping the inequalities \\(i\\neq j\\) at all \\(\\mathcal D\\)-layers. Keep their weights equal to \\(\\alpha^{-1}\\) and \\(\\beta\\) as in (23). These are assigned weights on the newly admitted tuples, not their geometric occupation moments. Every original tuple retains its weight, and every new weight is nonnegative, so this enlargement gives an upper bound.</p><p>Equality of matrix-entry coordinates equates the path labels at both ends of that layer. The relaxed equalities can therefore be counted independently at every internal vertex. A layer of type \\(\\mathcal N\\) pairs the paths as\n\\[\n\\{P,Q\\},\\{R,S\\},\n\\]\nwhile a layer of type \\(\\mathcal D\\) pairs them as\n\\[\n\\{P,R\\},\\{Q,S\\}.\n\\]\nAt a vertex between layers of the same type, there are two free labels in \\([n]\\). At a vertex between different types, the two pairings together identify all four labels, leaving one free label. These give \\(n^2\\) and \\(n\\) choices, respectively. The two external vertex labels are fixed to \\(1\\).</p><p>Every independent choice at the internal vertices gives four paths, because all matrix entries are available, and every relaxed quadruple arises uniquely this way. The assignments form a Cartesian product over internal vertices. Their number is exactly\n\\[\nn^{2L-w(\\tau)}.\n\\]</p><p>For a layer \\(t\\) of type \\(\\mathcal N\\), each of the \\(h_t\\) internal end vertices between two \\(\\mathcal N\\)-layers has two independent uniform labels in \\([n]\\), which agree with probability \\(1/n\\). At an external vertex or an interface between different types, the labels already agree. Consequently the two coordinates \\(i,j\\) of this layer agree with probability\n\\[\nn^{-h_t}.\n\\]</p><p>The end-vertex sets of different \\(V\\)-layers are disjoint, because no two \\(V\\)-layers are adjacent. Under the uniform distribution on the Cartesian product of relaxed vertex assignments, these equality events are therefore independent for the \\(\\mathcal N\\)-layers in \\(V\\). Their average weight is\n\\[\n\\prod_{t\\in N_V(\\tau)}\n\\bigl(1+\\alpha^{-1}n^{-h_t}\\bigr).\n\\]\nFor each \\(\\mathcal N\\)-layer in \\(U\\), instead bound its weight pointwise by \\(1+\\beta\\), so no independence assertion for these layers is needed.</p><p>Combining the occupation comparison, relaxed assignment count, and weight estimates proves (24).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is an absolute constant \\(C\\) such that, for all sufficiently large \\(n\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the layer schedule (12) and numerical parameters \\(\\alpha,\\beta,\\rho,k,m,s,b\\) in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\tau\\in\\{\\mathcal N,\\mathcal D\\}^n\\), let \\(w(\\tau)\\) be its number of switches, let \\(d_V(\\tau),d_U(\\tau)\\) count its \\(\\mathcal D\\)-positions in the respective variable groups, and let\n  \\[\n  N_V(\\tau)=\\{t:t\\text{ is in }V,\\ \\tau_t=\\mathcal N\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t\\in N_V(\\tau)\\), let \\(h_t\\) be the number of internal vertices adjacent to layer \\(t\\) whose two incident layers both have type \\(\\mathcal N\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute constant \\(C\\) such that, for all sufficiently large \\(n\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\tau\\in\\{\\mathcal N,\\mathcal D\\}^n}\nn^{-w(\\tau)}\n\\alpha^{-d_V(\\tau)}\n\\beta^{d_U(\\tau)}\n\\prod_{t\\in N_V(\\tau)}\n\\bigl(1+\\alpha^{-1}n^{-h_t}\\bigr)\n\\leq\\exp(C\\sqrt n).\n\\tag{25}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Denote the summand on the left of (25) by \\(W(\\tau)\\). Call a position isolated if it is an interior \\(V\\)-layer with type \\(\\mathcal N\\) and both its neighbors have type \\(\\mathcal D\\). Change every isolated position to type \\(\\mathcal D\\), obtaining a word \\(F(\\tau)\\). No two \\(V\\)-layers are adjacent, so none of their neighbors is changed. It follows that \\(F(\\tau)\\) has no isolated position, and the values of \\(h_t\\) at its remaining \\(N_V\\)-positions are unchanged.</p><p>Each changed position has \\(h_t=0\\) before the change. Its two incident switches disappear, its factor \\(1+\\alpha^{-1}\\) disappears, and a factor \\(\\alpha^{-1}\\) is introduced. Switches removed at distinct positions are distinct, even when those positions are separated by only one \\(U\\)-layer. Thus, if \\(j\\) positions were changed,\n\\[\n\\frac{W(\\tau)}{W(F(\\tau))}\n=\\left(\\frac{1+\\alpha}{n^2}\\right)^j.\n\\tag{26}\n\\]</p><p>The preimages can be counted exactly. For a word \\(y\\) with no isolated position, let \\(E(y)\\) be the set of interior \\(V\\)-positions whose type and both neighboring types are \\(\\mathcal D\\). Its preimages are obtained by changing an arbitrary subset of \\(E(y)\\) to \\(\\mathcal N\\). These positions are nonadjacent, their neighbors remain unchanged, and the operation creates precisely the isolated positions that are changed back by \\(F\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\tau:F(\\tau)=y}W(\\tau)\n=\nW(y)\\left(1+\\frac{1+\\alpha}{n^2}\\right)^{|E(y)|}\n\\leq W(y)(1+2/n^2)^k,\n\\]\nwhere \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1\\) and \\(|E(y)|\\leq k\\) were used.</p><p>In a word with no isolated position, every interior \\(N_V\\)-position has \\(h_t\\geq1\\). An endpoint position can have \\(h_t=0\\); there is at most one endpoint \\(V\\)-layer, since the schedule starts with \\(V\\) and ends with \\(U\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\sum_\\tau W(\\tau)\n\\leq\n(1+2/n^2)^k(1+\\alpha^{-1})(1+\\alpha^{-1}/n)^k Z,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nZ=\\sum_\\tau n^{-w(\\tau)}\n\\alpha^{-d_V(\\tau)}\\beta^{d_U(\\tau)}.\n\\tag{27}\n\\]\nHere the final sum has been enlarged from words without isolated positions to all words. By Proposition 1, \\(\\alpha^{-1}\\leq\\sqrt n\\) and \\(k\\leq n/2\\), so the logarithm of the prefactor is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{2k}{n^2}+\\log(1+\\sqrt n)+\\frac{k}{\\sqrt n}\n=O(\\sqrt n).\n\\]</p><p>It remains to bound \\(Z\\). A run of \\(\\mathcal D\\)-positions is a maximal nonempty interval of such positions. Each interior run has two switches, contributing \\(n^{-2}\\), whereas a run meeting exactly one endpoint has one switch. We will show that the product of layer weights on every run is at most \\(n\\) times a rounding factor. Thus the switch costs leave a factor \\(n^{-1}\\) for every interior run.</p><p>For an interval \\(I\\) of consecutive layers, define\n\\[\nd(I)=\\#\\{V\\text{-layers in }I\\}\n-\\rho\\,\\#\\{U\\text{-layers in }I\\}.\n\\]\nWe claim that\n\\[\nd(I)\\leq1+\\rho\\quad\\text{for every interval }I,\n\\qquad\nd([n])=0.\n\\tag{28}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(r\\) consecutive complete chunks of the schedule, starting after chunk \\(j\\), contain \\(r\\) layers in \\(V\\) and \\(r+E\\) layers in \\(U\\), where\n\\[\nE=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{(j+r)s}{k}\\right\\rfloor\n-\\left\\lfloor\\frac{js}{k}\\right\\rfloor,\n\\qquad\n|E-rs/k|&lt;1.\n\\]\nUsing \\(m=k+s\\) and \\(\\rho=k/m\\), their discrepancy is\n\\[\nr-\\rho(r+E)=\\rho(rs/k-E)&lt;\\rho.\n\\]</p><p>An interval spanning several chunks consists of consecutive complete chunks, possibly preceded by a proper terminal part of one chunk and followed by a proper initial part of another. The terminal part contains only \\(U\\)-layers and has nonpositive discrepancy; the initial part contains at most one \\(V\\)-layer and has discrepancy at most \\(1\\). An interval contained in a single chunk also has discrepancy at most \\(1\\). This proves the first assertion of (28). The second is the identity \\(k-\\rho m=0\\).</p><p>By Proposition 1, the product of layer weights on a \\(\\mathcal D\\)-run \\(I\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\alpha^{-\\#V(I)}\\beta^{\\#U(I)}\n\\leq\n\\alpha^{-d(I)}\\exp(C\\#U(I)/b)\n\\leq\nn\\exp(C\\#U(I)/b).\n\\]\nFor the last inequality, the sign of \\(d(I)\\) causes no difficulty:\n\\[\n\\alpha^{-d(I)}\n\\leq\\alpha^{-(1+\\rho)}\n\\leq n^{(1+\\rho)/2}\n\\leq n,\n\\]\nsince \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1\\), \\(\\alpha\\geq n^{-1/2}\\), and \\(\\rho&lt;1\\). The runs are disjoint, so all their rounding factors together contribute at most \\(\\exp(Cn/b)\\).</p><p>Consider a mixed word, meaning one containing both types. Let \\(r\\) be its number of interior \\(\\mathcal D\\)-runs and \\(e\\) its number of \\(\\mathcal D\\)-runs meeting an endpoint. A mixed word has no run meeting both endpoints, so \\(e\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\), and\n\\[\nw(\\tau)=2r+e.\n\\]\nThere are \\(r+e\\) runs. Their weight, including switch factors, is therefore at most\n\\[\nn^{-(2r+e)}n^{r+e}\\exp(Cn/b)\n=n^{-r}\\exp(Cn/b).\n\\]</p><p>The initial type and the set of switch positions uniquely specify a word. Thus at most\n\\[\n2\\binom{n-1}{2r+e}\n\\]\nwords have given values of \\(r,e\\). Taking binomial coefficients outside their usual range to be zero, the mixed-word contribution to \\(Z\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\exp(Cn/b)\n\\sum_{e=0}^2\\sum_{r\\geq0}\n2\\binom{n-1}{2r+e}n^{-r}\n\\leq\n6n^2\\exp(\\sqrt n+Cn/b).\n\\]\nTo see the last inequality, for each \\(e\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\), use\n\\[\n\\sum_{r\\geq0}\\binom{n-1}{2r+e}n^{-r}\n\\leq\n\\sum_{r\\geq0}\\frac{n^{r+e}}{(2r+e)!}\n\\leq\nn^2\\sum_{r\\geq0}\\frac{(\\sqrt n)^{2r}}{(2r)!}\n\\leq n^2\\exp(\\sqrt n).\n\\]</p><p>The constant word \\(\\mathcal N^n\\) has weight \\(1\\). The other constant word has weight\n\\[\n\\alpha^{-k}\\beta^m\n\\leq\n\\alpha^{-k+\\rho m}\\exp(Cm/b)\n=\\exp(Cm/b).\n\\]\nIt follows that\n\\[\nZ\\leq\n\\exp(Cn/b)\\bigl(2+6n^2\\exp(\\sqrt n)\\bigr)\n=\\exp(O(\\sqrt n)),\n\\]\nwhere \\(b=\\Theta(n^{5/2})\\). Combining this with (27) proves the lemma.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [3, 6, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [3, 11, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [6, 4, "cite"], [7, 0, "cite"], [7, 1, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [8, 10, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [10, 7, "cite"], [11, 7, "cite"]]});
